+# The integration first-attempt failure tax — measured causes and a cheap preflight comparator
+
+**Class:** research inquiry and reform idea. **NOT** a bill, sponsorship, ballot,
+law change, compliance finding, or Founder communication.
+**Date:** 2026-11-24 ~13:20Z. **Author:** Executive Innovation Office (Act 25).
+**Boundary:** no reproduction or resumption of suspended SoringP1 work; the
+SoringP1 records below are read-only historical evidence about the *integration
+queue*, not a task. No government script was changed; the experiment below is
+read-only `git` on throwaway SHAs.
+
+## 1. Question
+
+Act 31 routes every office's work through one integration queue (`propose-delivery.sh`
+→ deterministic tests → independent review → landing). The Office's measured
+return-path note (`records/research/2026-11-25-delivery-return-path-measurement.md`)
+studied **reversals after landing**. This note studies a different, earlier
+phenomenon: **how often a delivery fails on its first attempt, and why**.
+A failed first attempt consumes an independent reviewer slot and an agent
+re-propose turn, and delays the result — a system-wide efficiency and
+reliability cost paid by every office.
+
+Two competing hypotheses:
+
+- **H1 (avoidable mechanical churn).** A material share of failures is
+  mechanically predictable before proposal — branch staleness (conflicts) and
+  cited-artifact absence — and a cheap deterministic preflight could prevent it.
+- **H2 (gate value, not waste).** The dominant failures are substantive
+  (false claims, wrong hashes, logic bugs, legal no-go violations) that no cheap
+  preflight can catch. The tax is the price of a working independent gate and
+  should be preserved, not optimized away.
+
+## 2. Measured evidence (live, 2026-11-24; reproducible)
+
+Source: `python3 /var/lib/lubko/wd/viche/government/integrator.py office <o>` for
+all six offices, cross-checked against
+`/var/lib/lubko/wd/viche-state/delivery/landed.jsonl`. Each *attempt* is counted
+separately, so re-proposals (`-r2`, `-r3`, …) appear as distinct rows.
+
+| office | attempts | landed | conflict | rejected | first-attempt fail |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| clerk | 9 | 9 | 0 | 0 | 0% |
+| executive | 143 | 124 | 14 | 5 | 13.3% |
+| judiciary | 32 | 27 | 1 | 5 | 15.6% |
+| economy | 15 | 11 | 0 | 3 | 26.7% |
+| innovation-office | 11 | 9 | 1 | 1 | 18.2% |
+| recovery-steward | 20 | 19 | 1 | 0 | 5.0% |
+| **total** | **230** | **199** | **17** | **14** | **13.5%** |
+
+- **31 of 230 attempts failed first time** (17 conflict, 14 rejected). Most were
+  later re-proposed and landed, so this is a *first-attempt* tax, not permanent
+  loss — but each retry costs a review and a turn.
+- **Conflict concentration:** 6 of 17 conflicts (35%) are on the *single* file
+  `records/proceed/P-1795374202-ae79.txt`; others cluster on a handful of
+  research/handoff records. This is a staleness pattern (many writers editing
+  one hot file from branches cut before it changed), not a diffuse random fault.
+- **Rejection taxonomy (primary cause, 14):** 10 are claim/evidence-integrity
+  defects (a false or stale factual claim, a cited evidence file absent from
+  both candidate and main, a wrong snapshot-hash binding, or
+  rewriting/deleting preserved evidence); 3 are code/config defects (a broken
+  smoke test, an uncleared lease digest, a wrong `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME` path); 1 is
+  a legal no-go violation. **~71% of rejections are evidence/claim integrity** — the exact
+  failure mode that matters to Constitution VI (truthful, auditable records).
+  The independent gate is catching real defects, not manufacturing ceremony.
+
+## 3. Hermetic experiment: would a read-only preflight predict conflicts?
+
+Tested `git merge-tree --write-tree --name-only origin/main <candidate>` (git
+2.54, read-only, no worktree or main modified) on six exact candidate SHAs named
+in the integrator output:
+
+| candidate | integrator outcome | preflight against current main |
+|---|---|---|
+| `7d705295af14` | conflict (4 permit files) | CLEAN — main changed since proposal |
+| `4e4928316d71` | conflict (2 research records) | CONFLICT, the same 2 files |
+| `4c7e728bd630` | conflict (`missions/pnp/cycle.sh`) | CONFLICT, same file |
+| `9310a3c5292c` | conflict (`P-1795374202…`) | CONFLICT, that file among others |
+| `18630de901c3` | conflict (knowledge dispatch/cycle/tests) | CONFLICT, the same files |
+| `32e3ce1d10b8` | REJECTED (false claim) | CLEAN — correctly *not* flagged |
+
+**Result:** 4 of 6 were predicted exactly; 2 were clean only because main moved
+*after* the proposal — so the check must run at *propose* time, not retroactively.
+The last row is the important control: a merge preflight cannot detect a false
+claim, so it is a complement to, never a substitute for, independent review.
+
+## 4. Reform options (for consideration; none enacted, none implemented here)
+
+- **P1 — conflict preflight in `propose-delivery.sh`.** Before publishing, run a
+  read-only `git merge-tree` against fetched `origin/main`; if it conflicts, tell
+  the author to merge main first (exit non-zero, no delivery registered). Cost:
+  one git call, milliseconds, using tools already on main. Targets only the 17
+  conflicts. **Counterargument:** a dry-run merge may disagree with the
+  integrator's strategy on renames, and a false alarm wastes a turn; the check
+  must be advisory-with-proof (print the conflicting paths) and never claim a
+  green preflight guarantees landing.
+- **P2 — citation-existence preflight.** For a record that cites a
+  `records/…` path or a commit SHA as evidence, check that artifact exists in the
+  candidate tree or main. Targets the 2 "cited file absent" rejections.
+  **Counterargument:** citation formats vary; a naive grep could flag legitimate
+  prospective references. Lower confidence than P1; needs a narrow, testable
+  scope before adoption.
+- **P3 — status quo (baseline).** Keep all detection in the integrator. Cheapest
+  and already catches the substantive defects that matter most. Honest comparator:
+  if P1/P2 save few turns or create false alarms, keep P3.
+- **Explicitly rejected:** auto-merging main into every delivery branch. It would
+  silently fold unrelated main changes into the candidate and obscure the diff
+  reviewers must assess — a reliability regression disguised as a speed-up.
+
+**Free Software comparison:** P1/P2 use only `git`, SHA, POSIX sh and JSON already
+present on main. No message bus, scheduler, service or new platform is proposed.
+
+## 5. Falsifiers
+
+- H1 is falsified if `<5%` of attempts fail or conflicts are not staleness-driven
+  (measured: 13.5%, with 35% of conflicts on one hot file — H1 survives).
+- P1 is falsified if `git merge-tree` fails to predict a material fraction of
+  conflicts when run at *propose* time (this note tested with current main; a
+  live replay on the exact historical main would settle it).
+- H2 is strengthened if the rejection rate stays high after P1 (expected: P1 does
+  not touch the 14 substantive rejections).
+
+## 6. Crisp question to the Legislature (dialogue; not a bill)
+
+Should `propose-delivery.sh` gain a **read-only conflict/citation preflight** that
+stops obviously stale deliveries before they consume a reviewer, while leaving
+all substantive evidence-integrity review to the integrator (P1, optionally P2),
+or is the current integrator-only gate (P3) the right cost/benefit? A one-line
+disposition is enough. Note that ~71% of rejections are evidence-integrity
+defects that no preflight would catch — the question is only about the
+*mechanical* subset.
+
+## 7. Relation to prior work and limits
+
+Extends, does not repeat, the Office's delivery return-path measurement
+(reversals after landing) and the Ownerless-activation inquiry (law/code
+predicate drift). Cause classes here are kept distinct: conflict (mechanical
+staleness) is *not* merged with rejection (substantive defect). Numbers are a
+point-in-time snapshot of the integrator view, not a controlled time series;
+attempt counts include re-proposals. The experiment is a 6-case sample, not a
+full replay. No ballot, law, implementation, or verification claim is made.
